#let chapter = [

= Metric Graphs
Before defining metric graphs, we first cite a standard result about the relationship between length functions and distances on graphs.
#lem[Lengths induce Distances][
  Let $G = (V,E)$ be a connected, simple graph with a length function $ell: E -> RR_+$. It induces a metric $dist_ell$ on $V$ by taking the shortest path distances, that is: 
  $ dist_ell (u,v) := min_(P "is a path"\ "from "u" to "v) quad sum_(e in P) thick ell(e) quad quad quad "for all" u,v in V $
  If further $ell$ fulfills the triangle inequality, then $dist_ell$ extends $ell$ to $V$, that is, 
  $ dist_ell (u,v) = ell(u,v) quad quad "for all" u,v in V $
]

#def[Metric Graphs][
  Let $G = (V,E)$ be a finite, connected, simple graph with a length function $ell: E -> RR_+$ fulfilling the triangle inequality. Assume that $E$ is represented as an orientation, that is, a subset of $V times V$ such that at most one of $(u,v)$ and $(v,u)$ is in $E$.

  We create a set $Gamma$ by taking a copy of the closed interval $[0, ell(e)]$ for each edge $e=(u, v) in E$ and identifying the endpoints $0$ and $ell(e)$ with the vertices $u$ and $v$ respectively. 
  
  More formally, consider the disjoint union
  $ P := union.sq.big_(e in E) [0, ell(e)] $
  where we denote the point corresponding to $e in E$ and $t in [0, ell(e)]$ by $scp(e,t)$. We then define $Gamma := (P union V) slash ~$, where $~$ is an equivalence relation generated by the rules
  $
  &scp((u,v), 0) &&thick thick ~thick thick u quad quad && ("identification of the start vertex") \
  &scp((u,v), ell(u,v)) && thick thick ~thick thick v quad quad && ("identification of the end vertex") \
  $
  for all $(u,v) in E$. By abuse of notation we often refer to the equivalence class $[scp(e,t)]_~$ simply as $scp(e,t)$. Call any element not representable as a vertex an _edge point_.

  Two elements $x,y in Gamma$ are _adjacent_ if they can be represented by points $scp(e,t), scp(e,t') in [0, ell(e)]$ on the same edge. We can define a length function on this graph via the euclidean distance
  $d_E (x, y) := |t-t'|$. Define $d$ as the shortest path distance on $Gamma$ with respect to this length function. The resulting metric space $(Gamma, d)$ is called the _metric graph_ induced by $G$ and $ell$.
]

Note that the extension of $d_E$ to $d$ does indeed give a metric: Because $G$ is connected, any two points in $Gamma$ can be joined by a path.

We can restrict ourselves to the following paths in the future:

#def[Direct Paths][
  A path $P$ in $Gamma$ is _direct_ if all points except possibly start- and end-points are vertices.
]

